{"sdk_http_response":{"headers":{"x-gemini-service-tier":"standard","content-type":"application/json; charset=UTF-8","vary":"Origin, X-Origin, Referer","content-encoding":"gzip","date":"Fri, 27 Nov 2026 17:15:13 GMT","server":"scaffolding on HTTPServer2","x-xss-protection":"0","x-frame-options":"SAMEORIGIN","x-content-type-options":"nosniff","server-timing":"gfet4t7; dur=27446","alt-svc":"h3=\":443\"; ma=2592000,h3-29=\":443\"; ma=2592000","transfer-encoding":"chunked"},"body":null},"candidates":[{"content":{"parts":[{"media_resolution":null,"code_execution_result":null,"executable_code":null,"file_data":null,"function_call":null,"function_response":null,"inline_data":null,"text":"**Claim Grading Analysis Summary**\n\nOkay, here's the breakdown of my thought process for grading these claims. The task is to evaluate each extracted claim against the provided text, using the given rubric, and assign a grade. The key is to match the subject, relation, object, and modality in the claim to the corresponding content within the text.\n\nI'm starting by reading each claim. Then, I'm finding the relevant text section to compare. The rubric provides guidelines on the types of the Subject and Object, and the Relations that are considered correct. If there is a mismatch, the item is downgraded.\n\n*   **Claims 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 10, 12, 13, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 20:** These look straightforward. The subject, relation, object, and modality all appear to align correctly with the text. These appear faithful.\n\n*   **Claim 5:** This is a definition claim. The critical part is the direction: is the subject the *term* being defined or the *definition* itself? The rubric specifies the relation's direction is from definition to term. In this case, the subject is the *term* (division algebra), and the object is the *definition*. This is the wrong direction, so the grade is \"wrong-direction\".\n\n*   **Claim 6 and 9:** These involve a \"Proposition\" and a statement. The rubric stipulates that a result *proves* a statement. But the relation says \"equivalent-to\". The relation is wrong. \"Proposition 5\" is equivalent to \"A is nicely normed iff A' is nicely normed\", and Proposition 2 similarly. Since the subject is a result/proposition and the object is a statement, the relationship is wrong; it should be \"proves\". \"wrong-relation\".\n\n*   **Claim 8:** Similar to Claim 5, this is a definition, where the subject is the *term* \"alternative algebra\" and the object is the definition. Definition to term: The direction is wrong.\n\n*   **Claim 11:** The key here is the *modality*: Is the claim \"stated\" (the paper asserts it) or \"cited\" (attributed to an external source)? Theorem 2 is presented as \"Theorem 2. ...\", so it's *stated*, even though attributed. The paper itself gives a citation for Theorem 2: \"For modern proofs... see Schafer's\". So this is cited. I now understand that cited means, the paper doesn't assert it. Therefore the claim has `wrong-modality`.\n\n*   **Claim 14:**  Here, we have \"alternative algebra\" (a property or object) and \"identities\" (statements). \"equivalent-to\" requires matching types of either objects or statements. However, an alternative algebra implies the given identities. I need to be exact here. This relation is wrong, since the 'equivalent-to' relation type does not correctly match.\n\nThat's the process I'm following for each claim, and the reasoning behind each of my grades.\n\n\n","thought":true,"thought_signature":null,"video_metadata":null,"tool_call":null,"tool_response":null,"part_metadata":null,"audio_transcription":null,"media_processing":null},{"media_resolution":null,"code_execution_result":null,"executable_code":null,"file_data":null,"function_call":null,"function_response":null,"inline_data":null,"text":"{\"n\": 1, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"This was independently proved by Kervaire [ 62 ] and Bott–Milnor [ 11 ] in 1958.\"}\n{\"n\": 2, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"An associative algebra has multiplicative inverses iff it is a division algebra. However, this fails for nonassociative algebras! ... we can construct a division algebra without multiplicative inverses by taking the quaternions and modifying the product slightly, setting i^2=-1+\\\\epsilon j\"}\n{\"n\": 3, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Proposition 1: . A ′ is never real.\"}\n{\"n\": 4, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The compact real form of e6 is given by ... ≅ so(O) ⊕ so(C) ⊕ Im(C) ⊕ (C⊗O)^3\"}\n{\"n\": 5, \"grade\": \"wrong-direction\", \"reason\": \"An algebra A is a division algebra if given a,b ∈ A with ab=0, then either a=0 or b=0.\"}\n{\"n\": 6, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"Proposition 5: . A is nicely normed ⇔ A ′ is nicely normed.\"}\n{\"n\": 7, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The compact real form of f4 is given by f4 ≅ isom(OP2)\"}\n{\"n\": 8, \"grade\": \"wrong-direction\", \"reason\": \"It is alternative if the subalgebra generated by any two elements is associative.\"}\n{\"n\": 9, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"Proposition 2: . A is real (and thus commutative) ⇔ A ′ is commutative.\"}\n{\"n\": 10, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"An associative algebra has multiplicative inverses iff it is a division algebra. However, this fails for nonassociative algebras! ... we can construct a division algebra without multiplicative inverses by taking the quaternions and modifying the product slightly, setting i^2=-1+\\\\epsilon j\"}\n{\"n\": 11, \"grade\": \"wrong-modality\", \"reason\": \"A version of the second theorem appears in an 1930 paper by Zorn [ 100 ]\"}\n{\"n\": 12, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The compact real form of f4 is given by ... ≅ so(O) ⊕ O^3\"}\n{\"n\": 13, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The compact real form of e6 is given by ... ≅ der(h3(O)) ⊕ sh3(O)\"}\n{\"n\": 14, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"by a theorem of Emil Artin [ 84 ] , an algebra A is alternative iff for all a , b ∈ A we have (aa)b=a(ab), (ab)a=a(ba), (ba)a=b(aa)\"}\n{\"n\": 15, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Theorem 3: . All division algebras have dimension 1, 2, 4, or 8. This was independently proved by Kervaire [ 62 ] and Bott–Milnor [ 11 ] in 1958.\"}\n{\"n\": 16, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The compact real form of e8 is given by ... ≅ so(O) ⊕ so(O) ⊕ (O⊗O)^3\"}\n{\"n\": 17, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Proposition 3: . A is commutative and associative ⇔ A ′ is associative.\"}\n{\"n\": 18, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Theorem 1: . R, C, H, and O are the only normed division algebras. The first theorem goes back to an 1898 paper by Hurwitz [ 55 ] .\"}\n{\"n\": 19, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Proposition 5: . A is nicely normed ⇔ A ′ is nicely normed.\"}\n{\"n\": 20, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"A version of the second theorem appears in an 1930 paper by Zorn [ 100 ]\"}\nGRADES-END","thought":null,"thought_signature":"Eo5SCotSAVjVB3uuho8ghvBWW5Dy78JCTNFt8qxgDhJM2ja-DUncgsGhduXfQ0N59QLOObL3P-id6Lrr2AjJQbnbmTChaFXVcoPKStEk7BkSM7dy6hp5yTdACgiY3p-GTehb34yptBzgAdkPshU7b3y06uuT_EZyPQ60b4TU-GBttwsm54vD2c41DL_dSzHSDIShgzgPCC8yFGMGs0f9leIGyL-Th5wWzvYmUX74ZPhG3OvAvct8I9JvzW5am2mua2e6Gk-sYfw41I_qESEwqw59QZ1ow3vLO_cbjqDXuUXWCz0VEwY65ZYvB57voQ2xoUqQ65cjPG6V06Ex2refzs9cNy0jZvigqkQpW_L7To8gQ7YKa31RRwALHb_bqxqUUWnUUYfIfrInSkpSouVOBV3c0GEz__YzXngL4H_z6UyhT70vz72mwT8v28F1uvAXtzAjrQVXHcHMLvz9pdGlie1d2AT0t5DtWW73oi3ml_p1uEbmDnNlCJxwfk6zqYjnEZn_e6NOAZJw_aShrlm0sOuih5GItmW1PsphlFtxW-f70BPBGmQe2ON7o2KpVSncCyj8TRZeIw-HUYrSjkj0CqjxegnHlH4UFsdS72zvxhsud6l5FENrEDk3bmj8eYUFzkn49eCLCygbznEfaT-T76fhDcIhaOO6XQ8f1hec3IX53tA2l3ijNr0A95ezyqT0_kKZ9UQ-pGPJ-3NR2Wdr0ZfeQBkvjDVU1so0CbcI7E1ygwqLgP44ipSzbdGzuPsUz-IYAWntureLqSBul1bTHz3rOWltv-UU0LiM6xdqVBs1wmTGgleM2IzEpKQ_roPv6zDs35XivH3dBj9QHc7m5OhA8oxZcR8WXsMFMeLfNHUXAho9j_CZ7ksb6kqiaoFHbStqybFxdHwLF3g6YmU_QL8zer1Ls4KKXQVZnTuOpNj9g08KezPR8EHrLWYkgxFwnZQBO_HxiALMpR5mfscQv6drp9P9wVbJt